+# Implementation Plan: S004 Provider retry quota
+
+**Branch**: `S004-provider-retry` | **Date**: 2026-08-11 | **Spec**: [spec.md](spec.md)
+**Input**: Story specification from `specs/projects/001-pulseboard/epics/E002-reminders/stories/S004-provider-retry/spec.md`
+
+## Execution Flow (/story-plan command scope)
+
+1. Spec and available workspace context loaded; no clarification markers found.
+2. Architecture decision gate: pass with a focused transactional design because the story spans quota state, provider completion, cancellation, and concurrent callback paths.
+3. No outline, codebase scan, constitution, project context, UX artifact, or prior research report exists in this workspace.
+4. Phase 1 complete: data model, provider-boundary contract, and executable validation walkthrough are defined below and in their canonical artifacts.
+5. Stop here. `/story-tasks` may turn the requirements table into implementation tasks; `tasks.md` is intentionally absent.
+
+## Summary
+
+Provider retries use a durable reservation keyed by the sending attempt. The retry path atomically reserves a workspace credit before invoking the provider, then transitions that same reservation exactly once to `finalized` on acceptance or `restored` on provider failure, timeout, or cancellation. The design makes the reservation identifier the idempotency key for provider callbacks and keeps the usable balance non-negative under concurrent attempts.
+
+## Technical Approach
+
+Add the quota logic at the existing reminder-send orchestration boundary once source code is available; this repository contains no application source from which to name a concrete module. Persist a per-attempt reservation alongside the authoritative workspace retry balance, and execute reservation creation plus balance decrement in one datastore transaction or equivalent conditional write. Treat every completion signal as a compare-and-set transition of the reservation state, so only the first terminal signal can finalize or restore credit. Invoke the provider only after a reservation is durable, pass the attempt/reservation identifier through the provider integration, and route all non-accepted terminal outcomes through the same restore operation. This is deliberately a small state machine rather than a new quota framework: the specification requires one balance and one lifecycle with no evidence for a reusable abstraction.
+
+## Dependencies
+
+- Requires: an authoritative per-workspace retry-credit balance, durable transactional/conditional-write support, and the existing reminder provider send path.
+- Requires: a stable send-attempt identifier available to the provider callback, timeout handler, and cancellation handler.
+- Provides: an idempotent quota lifecycle that later retry-policy or observability work can call without reimplementing credit restoration.
+
+## Testing Strategy
+
+- Unit tests: reservation state transitions; duplicate terminal signals; invalid transitions; zero-credit rejection.
+- Integration tests: simultaneous reservation attempts against one workspace, provider acceptance, provider error, timeout, and cancellation against real transaction semantics.
+- Contract tests: all provider outcomes identify the same `attempt_id`; duplicate callback payloads return the prior terminal result without a second balance mutation.
+- Failure injection: interrupt after reservation and before provider completion, then deliver a timeout/cancellation signal and assert one restoration only.
+
